WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.820 --> 00:00:05.620
The immense popularity of Verbinski's
The Ring — the American remake of Japanese

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:11.520
horror film Ringu — created a tidal wave
of interest in Japanese horror in the United

00:00:11.530 --> 00:00:12.530
States.

00:00:12.530 --> 00:00:18.039
And despite mixed reviews of other remakes,
like 2004's The Grudge, American horror fans

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:23.740
will find plenty to scream about in these
original, underappreciated Japanese horror

00:00:23.769 --> 00:00:24.760
movies.

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:30.080
Whether it's body horror or supernatural curses
you're after, these films are definitely horror

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:32.060
classics.

00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:33.060
Creepy

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:39.200
Director Kiyoshi Kurosawa is a well-known
auteur in Japan, but his films have gone mostly

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:45.580
overlooked in the U.S. 2016's Creepy was no
different — despite receiving critical acclaim

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:47.500
from The New York Times.

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:52.840
What begins as a story about the creepy neighbor
next door evolves into an epic funhouse of

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:58.420
scares with one of the most visceral endings
in recent horror history.

00:00:58.420 --> 00:00:59.400
Audition

00:00:59.410 --> 00:01:04.830
Before the cult classic Ichi the Killer, prolific
director Takashi Miike made the horror movie

00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:06.010
Audition.

00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:10.670
You may have yet to cross paths with this
intense film, mostly because of the terrifying

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:12.910
reputation that precedes it.

00:01:12.910 --> 00:01:18.080
1999's Audition has gone down in horror lore
as one of the most disturbing horror films

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:19.130
ever made.

00:01:19.130 --> 00:01:24.400
Although it seems like a slow-burn mystery
movie at first, Audition's terrifying conclusion

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:27.420
is nothing short of relentless.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:28.450
Dark Water

00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:34.210
The poorly received 2005 American remake of
this movie starring Jennifer Connelly is probably

00:01:34.210 --> 00:01:38.220
the reason why the original doesn't get the
credit it deserves.

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:43.780
2002's Dark Water was directed by Hideo Nakata
of Ringu fame.

00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:48.710
And fans of that film will recognize some
familiar tropes, most notably the single mother

00:01:48.710 --> 00:01:53.380
and her child fighting to break a curse before
they get totally wrecked by a supernatural

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:54.560
force.

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:58.010
But Dark Water definitely isn't a repeat of
Ringu.

00:01:58.010 --> 00:02:01.950
It's got enough fresh scares to stand well
on its own.

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:03.450
Hausu

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:10.619
Undoubtedly one of the strangest horror films
ever made, Nobuhiko Obayashi's Hausu defies

00:02:10.619 --> 00:02:11.780
categorization.

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:16.730
A group of friends travel to an aunt's haunted
mansion filled with an Alice in Wonderland-style

00:02:16.730 --> 00:02:22.750
cast of spirits, including a floating, demonic
Cheshire cat and a piano that likes to eat

00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:23.750
people.

00:02:23.750 --> 00:02:27.870
Released in 1977, Hausu is now considered
a horror classic.

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:33.630
Not bad for a movie that was inspired by Obayashi's
11-year-old daughter.

00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:35.030
Battle Royale

00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:39.569
The Hunger Games often draws comparisons to
Battle Royale, and for good reason.

00:02:39.569 --> 00:02:44.260
There are plenty of differences, but both
stories focus on a dystopian world in which

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:47.680
children compete in televised fights to the
bitter end.

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:51.819
Battle Royale is just...way more violent.

00:02:51.819 --> 00:02:57.170
Based on a 1999 Japanese novel of the same
name, the film adaptation of Battle Royale

00:02:57.170 --> 00:03:02.900
was one of the highest-grossing films in Japan,
raking in nearly $1.9 million in its opening

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:04.120
weekend.

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:08.740
Despite its success overseas, Battle Royale
wasn't officially distributed in the U.S.

00:03:08.740 --> 00:03:14.290
for nearly 11 years because of the controversy
surrounding its violent content.

00:03:14.290 --> 00:03:15.569
Jigoku

00:03:15.569 --> 00:03:20.560
It's hard to believe that this classic Japanese
horror film was released in 1960.

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:23.040
It feels as if it could have been made yesterday.

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:27.820
When a student flees the scene of a hit-and-run,
he's not only pursued by his guilty conscience,

00:03:27.820 --> 00:03:31.210
but also by a demonic double of himself.

00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:37.550
"Jigoku" translates to "hell," and director
Nobuo Nakagawa takes the viewer literally

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:44.150
to the fiery pits, making Jigoku a horror
masterpiece for the ages.

00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:46.480
Tetsuo: The Iron Man

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:51.459
Another J-horror classic, Tetsuo: The Iron
Man feels custom-made for fans of the weird

00:03:51.459 --> 00:03:57.590
body horror of David Cronenberg or the fleshier
David Lynch pictures like Eraserhead.

00:03:57.590 --> 00:04:03.470
Released in 1989, Tetsuo is about a man who
begins growing metal body parts after he accidentally

00:04:03.470 --> 00:04:05.810
takes out a freaky half-metal man.

00:04:05.810 --> 00:04:11.860
Nearly 30 years later, Tetsuo is still hard
to watch, making it one of horror's most overlooked

00:04:11.860 --> 00:04:12.860
classics.

00:04:12.860 --> 00:04:13.860
